---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';

const pageTitle = "Credits - Thamara Kandabada";
const slashpageImage = "/featured-images/credits.png";
---
<PageLayout title={pageTitle} image={slashpageImage}>

    <h1>Credits</h1>
    
    <p>I'm using the following tools to create and maintain this website. A number of them are open source projects created by indepenedent developers.</p>
    
    <div class="see-also">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>See also:</strong></li>
        <li><a href="/colophon">Colophon</a></li>
        <li><a href="/changelog">Changelog</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    
    <h2><a href="https://astro.build/" target="_blank">Astro</a></h2>
    <p>Astro is a JavaScript web framework that renders files server-side and sends lightweight HTML files to your browser, enabling a fast and satisfying browsing experience. I write the web pages in HTML, the blog posts in Markdown, and style them with CSS, which Astro's powerful templating engine converts to what you see here. Astro is completely free and has extensive beginner-friendly documentation.</p>
    <p>Their <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/tutorial/0-introduction/" target="_blank">Build a blog</a> tutorial and <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/getting-started/" target="_blank">Astro Docs</a> have been particularly useful.</p>

    <h2><a href="https://github.com/" target="_blank">GitHub</a></h2>
    <p>GitHub allows the source code written in Astro to be stored and deployed. It keeps track of the various versions of the code, with extensive records on each change made.</p>

    <h2><a href="https://vercel.com/home" target="_blank">Vercel</a></h2>
    <p>Vercel provides the cloud-based server infrastructure for this website to be deployed on. I'm also using their analytics and pagespeed insights tools to improve the browsing experience. I'm currently on Vercel's generous free tier, which offers more than enough computing power for a small personal website.</p>
    
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/karthikeyankc/discuss" target="_blank">Discuss</a></h2>
    <p>Discuss is a lighweight comment engine for static sites, created by <a href="https://karthikeyankc.com/" target="_blank">Karthikeyan KC</a>. I use it to power my Guestbook and the comments on my blogposts.</p>
  
    <h2><a href="https://www.digitalocean.com/" target="_blank">Digital Ocean</a></h2>
    <p>Discuss is a self-hosted tool, so I use a basic VPS from Digital Ocean (called a Droplet) to host the comments for this site. It is effective and easy to set up, and quite affordable.</p>

    <h2><a href="https://icons.getbootstrap.com/" target="_blank">Bootstrap Icons</a></h2>
    <p>All icons used on this website are from the open-source Bootstrap Icon Library. I am using them both as SVGs and web fonts.</p>

    <h2><a href="https://renata.haliya.net/thelab.html" target="_blank">Renata's Lab</a></h2>
    <p>This is a wonderfull collection of CSS snippets by Renata, a self-described "enthusiast of all things delightful."</p>

    <h2 id="commit-message-conventions"><a href="https://brennan.day/colophon/#commit-conventions" target="_blank">Commit Message Conventions</a></h2>
    <p>I use the commit message conventions developed by <a href="https://brennan.day/" target="_blank">Brennan Kenneth Brown</a>. All commit messages follow this format: <Code code=`<type>: <description> [optional body]` inline /></p>
    <p>Here is a full list of the types of commits and explainations of when they're used.</p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>Usage</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`post:` inline /></td>
        <td>A new essay, blog post, or other written content is published</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`edit:` inline /></td>
        <td>Corrections, revisions, or expansions to existing written content</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`update:` inline /></td>
        <td>Routine data refreshes like comments, uses page, now page, blogroll, podroll</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`design:` inline /></td>
        <td>Visual, layout, or CSS changes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`build:` inline /></td>
        <td>Site infrastructure, config, tooling, or dependency changes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`fix:` inline /></td>
        <td>Code bugs only; prose corrections use <Code code=`edit:` inline /> instead</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`ci:` inline /></td>
        <td>CI/CD pipeline changes (GitHub CI, Vercel config)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`a11y:` inline /></td>
        <td>Accessibility improvements like ARIA, contrast, alt text, semantics</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`perf:` inline /></td>
        <td>Performance improvements like build speed, asset size, caching</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`refactor:` inline /></td>
        <td>Code restructuring without behaviour change</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><Code code=`revert:` inline /></td>
        <td>Undoing a previous commit</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
    <h2><a href="https://webmention.io/" target="_blank">Webmention.io</a></h2>
    <p>This tool acts as an inbox that captures all incoming webmentions for this site. I display webmentions on my blogposts; they are populated on each build.</p>
    <h2><a href="https://webmention.app/" target="_blank">Webmention.app</a></h2>
    <p>Outgoing webmentions are automated using this tool, which scans the latest entry in my <a href="/notebook/rss.xml">Notebook feed</a> for valid endpoints and sends webmentions on each build.</p>
    <h2><a href="https://ritual.sh/resources/button-generator/" target="_blank">88x31 Button Maker</a></h2>
    <p>The "Made with CSS" and "Made with HTML" buttons in my footer were made using this helpful tool created by <a href="https://ritual.sh/" target="_blank">Dan Baker</a>.</p>

  </PageLayout>
